.TH LPS 1 2026-10-18 "Peios authd 0.0.24"
.SH NAME
lps \- administer the Peios local principal store
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lps
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lps
is the administration client for
.BR lpsd (8).
It does not open the principal database itself; every operation is authorized
and performed by the daemon over
.IR /run/lpsd/admin.sock .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B list
List every principal.
.TP
.BI show " name"
Show one principal in full.
.TP
.B domain
Print the machine's domain SID.
.TP
.BI add " [name] [options]"
Create a principal.  Options are
.BR \-\-group ,
.BR \-\-primary-group ,
.BR \-\-home ,
.BR \-\-shell ,
.BR \-\-display-name ,
.BR \-\-disabled ,
.BR \-\-no-password ,
.BR \-\-service ,
and
.BR \-\-no-prompt .
The principal is created whole, in one request, or not at all.
.B \-\-service
makes a principal that may sign on only as a service, started by the service
manager without a credential.
.TP
.BI remove " name"
Delete a principal.  RIDs are never reused; disabling an account is usually
safer because descriptors may continue to name its SID.
.TP
.BI rename " name new-name"
Rename a principal.  Its SID, groups and home directory stay; it signs in by
the new name.  A name held by another principal or a local group is refused.
.TP
.BI enable " name"
Permit a principal to log on.
.TP
.BI disable " name"
Refuse logon while retaining the principal and SID.
.TP
.BI logon-types " name type ..."
Set the kinds of sign-on the principal may be used for:
.BR interactive ,
.BR remote-interactive ,
.BR network ,
.BR network-cleartext ,
.BR batch ,
.BR new-credentials ,
.BR service ,
or
.B default
for the machine's default, which is every kind a person uses and never
.BR service .
.TP
.BI password " name"
Set a principal's password, as an administrator.  A principal changes their
own with
.BR passwd (1).
Setting material does not change credential policy.
.TP
.BI key\ list " name"
Show the credential policy, then each enrolled key's ID, SHA256 fingerprint,
the day it was added (UTC) and its label.
.TP
.BI key\ add " name public-key-file [label]"
Enroll an Ed25519 or RSA (3072 to 8192 bits) public key. Duplicate keys, key
options, certificates and private keys are refused. Enrollment does not change policy.
.TP
.BI key\ remove " name key-id"
Remove an enrolled key by its 32-digit hexadecimal ID. Removing the last key
does not enable password or no-credential access. Existing sessions remain live.
.TP
.BI policy " name password|key|either|none|denied"
Select explicitly which credentials may authenticate this principal.
The none policy allows access without a credential and is intended only for
explicit development fixtures. Missing permitted material always fails closed.
.TP
.BI set " name [options]"
Change profile fields using
.BR \-\-home ,
.BR \-\-shell ,
.BR \-\-display-name ,
or
.BR \-\-primary-group .
.TP
.B group list
List local groups, with their descriptions where any has one.
.TP
.BI group\ create " name [description]"
Create a local group, and say what it is for.  Anyone may read a group's
description.
.TP
.BI group\ rename " name new-name"
Rename a local group.  Its SID and members stay.
.TP
.BI group\ describe " name description"
Set a local group's description; an empty one clears it.
.TP
.BI group\ delete " name"
Delete a local group nobody is in and nobody has as their primary group.
.TP
.BI group\ add " name group"
Add a principal to a group.
.TP
.BI group\ remove " name group"
Remove a principal from a group.
.TP
.BI claim\ set " name claim type [value ...]"
Set a claim.  Supported types are int64, uint64, boolean, string, sid, and
octet.
.TP
.BI claim\ remove " name claim"
Remove a claim.
.SH INPUT AND NAMES
Group arguments accept a well-known name, a qualified built-in name, a local
group name, or a literal SID. Names are matched without case by lpsd;
well-known names take precedence over local group names.
.PP
Interactive account creation asks for omitted fields and sends no request
until confirmation. Supplied options skip their corresponding questions.
.B \-\-no-prompt
refuses missing required input. Without a terminal, password input is one
line rather than an echoed command-line argument.
.PP
A local account defaults to /home/name and /bin/sh, with Authenticated Users
as its primary group. No per-user group is created. Home and shell profile
paths must be absolute; recording a new home path does not move files.
.PP
The UID and GID displays use the source's effective numeric range. A dash
means no usable numeric range was assigned, not that the principal has no SID.
A local group member count counts only principals held by this store.
.PP
Claim names are matched without case. A claim can contain multiple values
or zero values; an empty claim differs from an absent claim. Boolean values
accept true, yes, or 1, and false, no, or 0. Octet values contain pairs of
ASCII hexadecimal digits. Changed claims affect newly issued tokens.

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command completed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
The request failed, was refused, or could not reach the daemon.
.TP
.B 2
The command arguments or input were invalid.
.PP
A transport failure after submission does not prove that a change was not
saved. Read back inspectable state before retrying a mutation.

.SH SECURITY
.B \-\-no-password
creates a principal that authenticates with no credential.  It is intended for
explicit disposable-image bootstrap policy, not normal production accounts.
.PP
The last-administrator guard counts enabled accounts with administrator
membership. It does not verify that another counted account has usable
credentials, permitted logon types, or a reachable sign-in route.
Keep a verified administrator session while changing access.
.PP
.BR remove ,
.BR disable ,
.B group remove
from Administrators,
.B logon-types
that allow none of interactive, remote-interactive or network sign-on,
.B policy
that leaves nothing to sign in with, and
.B key remove
of the only key of one who signs in only by key, are all refused for the last
enabled administrator.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR login (1),
.BR authd (8),
.BR lpsd (8)
